WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.138
Certificates are the deliberate
exception, and the reason is volume.

2
00:00:04.138 --> 00:00:08.793
Public TLS lifetimes are shrinking
from three hundred ninety-eight

3
00:00:08.793 --> 00:00:12.713
days to forty-seven by 2029,
so renewals come continuously.

4
00:00:12.713 --> 00:00:15.672
Putting each one through an
approval wouldn't add rigor;

5
00:00:15.672 --> 00:00:18.825
it would just teach approvers
to click yes without reading.

6
00:00:18.825 --> 00:00:23.056
So routine renewal runs on
protocol and renewal engines,

7
00:00:23.056 --> 00:00:25.487
with policy partly living in DNS.

8
00:00:25.487 --> 00:00:29.198
The anomalies still come through
the front door, like a new issuing

9
00:00:29.198 --> 00:00:33.313
authority or an unknown issuer, because
they're rare and they need a person.

10
00:00:33.313 --> 00:00:36.380
Speaking of the front door,
there should only be one.
